"""run a coding agent inside a workspace

The workspace name comes first; everything after it is the agent's, verbatim.

  wk ai claude bb4 -r          resume, in bb4
  wk ai pi bb4 --help          pi's own help, not this one
  wk ai claude                 in a workspace: this one, no name
  wk ai claude --dry-run bb4   the session it would run

The agent runs with relaxed permissions, after `wk doctor`'s checks of the workspace's boundary pass; git commits are
walled off for the session, a push is refused by the push service and a GitHub or Bugzilla write by the injector to
anything the session starts. The one exception is a wk-tools workspace, whose repository an agent commits and pushes
itself. Inside a workspace (`claude` there calls this) a key reaching it, or a write not refused, stops the session and
no --force crosses it.

A Claude session on a terminal starts with Remote Control on, named after the workspace, using the claude.ai login the
credential injector holds (`wk key set claude-login`); on a build box, whose `claude setup-token` credential Remote
Control refuses, it starts without it and says so.

The agents are installed by `wk new` (a macOS guest: at its first start); a workspace made without one is refused,
naming the remedy.
"""
from wk import ai
from wk.decl import Command

COMMAND = Command(name="required", ready=True, tail="all", session=True, group="workspaces", subs={"claude": {}, "pi": {}})


def main(args, inv, env=None, reg=None):
    return ai.main(args.positionals[0], args.tail, env, reg)
